-### Subtopics
-#### [Blinn-Phong Shading Model](2239-blinn-phong-shading-model.md)
-Uses the halfway vector instead of the reflected vector for specular calculation. Faster to compute (no reflection vector calculation) and often produces smoother highlights. Choose when performance is critical or smoother highlights are desired.
+**Parameters:**
+*   **Shininess ($\alpha$ or $n$):** Controls the size of the specular highlight. Default values in implementations often range from 10 to 64 [S1][S4, unofficial]. For a perfect reflector, the exponent is infinite [S1].
+*   **Coefficients ($k_a, k_d, k_s$):** Typically in the range [0, 1]. Some implementations use values > 1 for specular to simulate energy gain (non-physical) [S1][S4, unofficial].
+*   **Colors ($O_a, O_d, O_s$):** RGB vectors. Simplified implementations may assume $O_a = O_d$ and $O_s$ is a blend of $O_d$ and white [S1].
 
-#### [Phong Shading Model for Volume Data](1353-phong-shading-model-for-volume-data.md)
-Adapts Phong shading specifically for volume rendering using gradient-based normals and potentially specialized lookup tables for illumination modulation. Choose when rendering scalar fields where gradient magnitude affects appearance.
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Back-facing surfaces:** The diffuse term must be clamped to 0 when $N \cdot L < 0$ to prevent light from shining through the back of the object [S7, unofficial].
+*   **Non-physical results:** The model does not conserve energy; the sum of reflected light can exceed incident light if coefficients are not carefully tuned [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Vector normalization:** Failure to normalize $N, L, V$ before dot products results in incorrect shading intensity [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Coordinate spaces:** Vectors must be in the same space. Common errors include mixing model-space normals with world-space light directions [S2, unofficial].
 
-#### [Specular Reflections via Polynomial Expansion](1172-specular-reflections-via-polynomial-expansion.md)
-Approximates specular highlights using polynomial expansions. Choose when analytical integration of specular terms is required or for specific optimization paths.
+## Subtopics
+
+### Blinn-Phong Shading Model
+Uses the halfway vector $H = \frac{L+V}{|L+V|}$ instead of the reflection vector $R$. It is computationally cheaper (no reflection vector calculation) and produces slightly different highlight shapes. Choose when performance is critical or the specific highlight shape of Blinn-Phong is desired [2239-blinn-phong-shading-model.md].
+
+### Blinn-Phong Lighting with Depth Cueing
+Extends Blinn-Phong with depth-based fading to simulate atmospheric effects. Choose for scenes requiring depth-based visibility cues [2448-blinn-phong-lighting-with-depth-cueing.md].
+
+### Phong Shading Model for Volume Data
+Adapts the Phong model for volume rendering by using gradient vectors as normals and lookup tables for illumination. Choose for ray-casted volume visualization [1353-phong-shading-model-for-volume-data.md].
+
+### Specular Reflections via Polynomial Expansion
+Approximates the specular term using polynomial expansions for faster computation. Choose when hardware does not support efficient power functions or for specific optimization constraints [1172-specular-reflections-via-polynomial-expansion.md].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per sample point [G]. Requires a few dot products, a power operation, and vector arithmetic [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ for standard implementation [G]. VolumePro variant requires memory for reflectance maps (cube-mapped tables) [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Very fast, suitable for real-time rendering. The power operation is the most expensive part but is hardware-accelerated [G].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, visually pleasing for glossy surfaces, widely supported [G].
-*   **Limitations:** Not physically based (energy conservation not guaranteed), specular highlights can be sharp or unrealistic depending on shininess, does not account for view-dependent diffuse effects (BRDF) [G].
-*   **When to use:** General-purpose shading for implicit surfaces or volume data where physical accuracy is not paramount [G].
-*   **When not to use:** Physically based rendering (PBR) pipelines, or when view-dependent diffuse effects are needed [G].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per light source per fragment. The cost is dominated by a few dot products, a power function, and vector arithmetic.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ for material and light parameters. No large tables are required for the standard model [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Extremely fast, suitable for real-time rendering on CPUs and GPUs [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, computationally inexpensive, visually pleasing for many materials [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Not physically based (does not conserve energy, incorrect falloff for point lights unless manually added), specular highlights are view-dependent in a way that can look unrealistic for rough surfaces, no shadowing or ambient occlusion included [S2, S7, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Real-time applications where performance is critical and physical accuracy is not required. Good for stylized rendering or as a baseline lighting model.
+*   **When not to use:** Physically based rendering (PBR) pipelines, scenes requiring accurate energy conservation, or when realistic shadowing and global illumination are needed.
 
 ## Background
-The Phong shading model was introduced by Bui Tuong Phong in 1975 [S4, implied by FK03 reference, unofficial]. It is a local illumination model that approximates the reflection of light on surfaces [S2, unofficial]. It has been widely adopted in computer graphics for its simplicity and visual quality [G]. In volume rendering, it has been implemented in hardware systems like VolumePro, which uses pre-computed reflectance maps to accelerate the calculation of diffuse and specular contributions based on gradient and reflection vectors [S3, unofficial].
+The Phong shading model was developed by Bui Tuong Phong in 1975 [S2, unofficial]. It was introduced as an empirical model to approximate the way light reflects off surfaces, specifically focusing on the specular highlight. It became a standard in computer graphics due to its simplicity and visual effectiveness. It is widely used in real-time rendering engines, educational contexts, and as a component in more complex lighting pipelines [S1][S2, unofficial]. The model is often implemented in fragment shaders in OpenGL and Direct3D pipelines [S2, unofficial].
 
